  "worked_solution": "We summarize all the given information in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘\\,\\,⌢$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗\\,\\,⌣$ |\n\nNow, let's sketch our graph: \n\n- We draw a point on the vertical line $x=1$, where the derivative is undefined.\n\n- Since $x=1$ is a local minimum, the graph must go up to the left and to the right of that point.\n\n- The graph must be concave down on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and concave up on $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Finally, we are told that the function is continuous.\n\nTherefore, a possible graph of $f$ could be as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14188718/assets/0b08e02b0999da13ec71b2fee4e2df4a8754f54d99cb711e6ff19c7a81ace9de.png)",
